    {
      "number": 2,
      "slug": "valid-anagram",
      "title": "Valid Anagram",
      "category": "Arrays & Hashing",
      "leetcode_number": 242,
      "neetcode_name": "Valid Anagram",
      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "status": "Solved",
      "youtube_id": "9UtInBqnCgA",
      "problem_statement": "Given two strings <code>s</code> and <code>t</code>, return <code>true</code> if the two strings are anagrams of each other, otherwise return <code>false</code>. An anagram is a string that contains the exact same characters as another string, but the order of the characters can be different.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "input": "s = \"racecar\", t = \"carrace\"",
          "output": "true"
        },
        {
          "input": "s = \"jar\", t = \"jam\"",
          "output": "false"
        }
      ],
      "core_idea": {
        "goal": "Determine whether two strings are anagrams of each other.",
        "key_insight": "Two strings are anagrams exactly when they contain the same characters the same number of times — order doesn't matter, so the problem reduces to comparing character frequency counts instead of comparing the strings directly.",
        "trigger_phrase": "“Same letters, different order?” → reach for a character frequency count (Hash Map)."
      },
      "optimal_solution": {
        "data_structure": "Two Hash Maps (Python <code>dict</code>) tracking character counts",
        "steps": [
          "If <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> have different lengths, they can't be anagrams — return <code>False</code> immediately.",
          "Walk both strings together in one pass, building <code>counterS</code> and <code>counterT</code> with <code>dict.get(char, 0) + 1</code>.",
          "For every character in <code>counterS</code>, compare its count against <code>counterT.get(char, 0)</code>; any mismatch means the strings aren't anagrams — return <code>False</code>.",
          "If every character's count matches, return <code>True</code>."
        ],
        "time_complexity": "O(n + m)",
        "space_complexity": "O(1)"
      },
      "code": "class Solution:\n    def isAnagram(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n        counterS, counterT = {}, {}\n        if len(s) != len(t):\n            return False\n\n        for ch in range(len(s)):\n            counterS[s[ch]] = 1 + counterS.get(s[ch], 0)\n            counterT[t[ch]] = 1 + counterT.get(t[ch], 0)\n\n        for c in counterS:\n            if counterS[c] != counterT.get(c, 0):\n                return False\n\n        return True",
      "walkthrough": [
        "Two strings are anagrams exactly when every character appears the same number of times in both — order doesn't matter, so this calls for frequency counts rather than a position-by-position comparison.",
        "First check: if the lengths differ, the strings can't be anagrams, so return <code>False</code> immediately without doing any counting — a cheap short-circuit.",
        "Build two frequency dictionaries in a single pass over both strings together (same index <code>ch</code>), incrementing each character's count with <code>.get(char, 0) + 1</code> so a character seen for the first time defaults to 0 instead of raising a <code>KeyError</code>.",
        "Then compare: walk every key in <code>counterS</code> and check it has the same count in <code>counterT</code> (again using <code>.get</code>, so a character missing from <code>t</code> just reads as 0 and fails the comparison instead of throwing). If every count lines up, the strings are anagrams.",
        "Because <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> only contain lowercase English letters (per the problem's constraints), each dictionary never holds more than 26 keys — so despite looking like O(n) extra space, it's effectively O(1)."
      ],
      "mental_shortcut": "“Same letters, same counts → anagram.”",
      "related_problems": [
        "Group Anagrams",
        "Two Sum",
        "Contains Duplicate"
      ],
      "interview_talking_point": "I'm comparing character frequency counts instead of sorting, which keeps this at O(n) time instead of O(n log n) — and because the alphabet is fixed at 26 lowercase letters, the extra space is effectively O(1).",
      "edge_cases": [
        "Different lengths → return False immediately, no counting needed",
        "s and t are identical strings → trivially anagrams",
        "All identical characters, e.g. \"aaa\" vs \"aaa\" → true",
        "Input is lowercase-only per constraints — mixed case or Unicode input would need normalizing first"
      ],
      "alternative_solutions": [
        {
          "name": "Pythonic one-liner — collections.Counter",
          "note": "Same O(n) idea, but lets <code>Counter</code> do the counting and the comparison in one expression. Easiest to write correctly under interview pressure, and reads cleanly — the main trade-off is it's a library call instead of showing your own counting logic, so some interviewers may ask you to implement it by hand anyway (the primary solution above).",
          "code": "from collections import Counter\n\nclass Solution:\n    def isAnagram(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n        if len(s) != len(t):\n            return False\n        return Counter(s) == Counter(t)",
          "time_complexity": "O(n + m)",
          "space_complexity": "O(1)"
        },
        {
          "name": "Sorting",
          "note": "The simplest to reason about: two strings are anagrams exactly when sorting both produces the same sequence. Easier to explain out loud and needs no extra data structure, but it's slower — O(n log n) instead of O(n) — so it's a good fallback if you blank on the hash map approach, not the first choice.",
          "code": "class Solution:\n    def isAnagram(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n        return sorted(s) == sorted(t)",
          "time_complexity": "O(n log n)",
          "space_complexity": "O(n)"
        }
      ],
